00:01:35.494 --> 00:01:36.910
LIZ FONG-JONES:
So first of all, I

00:01:36.910 --> 00:01:39.340
wanted to lead off by telling
you about how does SRE

00:01:39.340 --> 00:01:41.050
help you improve reliability.

00:01:41.050 --> 00:01:43.270
But before I even do that,
I kind of want to lead off

00:01:43.270 --> 00:01:45.019
by talking about why
should you care about

00:01:45.019 --> 00:01:48.800
reliability, why should
you care about operations.

00:01:48.800 --> 00:01:51.204
And this may seem obvious
to some of you who

00:01:51.204 --> 00:01:52.370
have done operations before.

00:01:52.370 --> 00:01:54.850
To others of you, it
may be a new concept

00:01:54.850 --> 00:01:57.005
that as people who
develop software,

00:01:57.005 --> 00:01:58.630
we tend to spend a
lot of time thinking

00:01:58.630 --> 00:02:00.490
about when does my feature ship.

00:02:00.490 --> 00:02:02.610
OK, you know, I spent a
month shipping my feature.

00:02:02.610 --> 00:02:05.740
I spent six months
shipping my feature.

00:02:05.740 --> 00:02:07.310
What happens afterwards?

00:02:07.310 --> 00:02:09.539
And it turns out that if
you spend a month developing

00:02:09.539 --> 00:02:13.090
your feature, it might take a
month's worth of support work

00:02:13.090 --> 00:02:17.170
later, or it might even
take 10 times as much.

00:02:17.170 --> 00:02:19.511
You might wind up
spending-- for every month

00:02:19.511 --> 00:02:21.010
that you spend
developing a feature,

00:02:21.010 --> 00:02:24.890
it may end up having someone
spend a year supporting it.

00:02:24.890 --> 00:02:27.967
So it's really, really critical
that we think about how do we

00:02:27.967 --> 00:02:29.800
improve the interoperability
of our services

00:02:29.800 --> 00:02:33.050
to lower the cost of
ownership of our services.

00:02:33.050 --> 00:02:36.050
So SRE and DevOps try
to solve this problem.

00:02:36.050 --> 00:02:38.610
So why is this problem so bad?

00:02:38.610 --> 00:02:41.410
If you think about the way that
we used to operate services--

00:02:41.410 --> 00:02:43.600
I was a sys admin way
back in the day, right?

00:02:43.600 --> 00:02:45.430
We used to have a
world in which people

00:02:45.430 --> 00:02:47.770
who are product developers
wanted to ship features

00:02:47.770 --> 00:02:49.492
as quickly as possible.

00:02:49.492 --> 00:02:50.950
And then you had
operators who were

00:02:50.950 --> 00:02:54.020
told, keep the system
as stable as possible.

00:02:54.020 --> 00:02:55.810
We want 100%.

00:02:55.810 --> 00:02:58.385
And these two interests
are in diametric opposition

00:02:58.385 --> 00:02:59.260
to each other, right?

00:02:59.260 --> 00:03:01.690
You have product developers
who are speaking the software

00:03:01.690 --> 00:03:02.890
engineering language,
who are saying,

00:03:02.890 --> 00:03:03.970
why is my feature not out yet?

00:03:03.970 --> 00:03:06.310
And you have operators that are
saying my boss will punish me

00:03:06.310 --> 00:03:07.630
if I don't get 100% up time.

00:03:07.630 --> 00:03:09.550
You can't ship that feature.

00:03:09.550 --> 00:03:11.750
So that's not a
very healthy state.

00:03:11.750 --> 00:03:14.510
So how do we get
out of this state?

00:03:14.510 --> 00:03:16.360
The good news is we
have some lessons

00:03:16.360 --> 00:03:18.897
that we've learned from
the practice of agile,

00:03:18.897 --> 00:03:20.980
from the practice of making
sure that your product

00:03:20.980 --> 00:03:23.200
management and your
software engineering teams

00:03:23.200 --> 00:03:25.150
are on the same page
as each other, right?

00:03:25.150 --> 00:03:26.290
That's what agile is about.

00:03:26.290 --> 00:03:28.810
Agile is about kind of
reducing that barrier,

00:03:28.810 --> 00:03:32.770
reducing friction, shipping
smaller increments faster.

00:03:32.770 --> 00:03:34.480
And the good news
is that DevOps is

00:03:34.480 --> 00:03:36.790
a way of solving this
problem and solving

00:03:36.790 --> 00:03:39.430
this wall between
development and your software

00:03:39.430 --> 00:03:41.110
operations team, right?

00:03:41.110 --> 00:03:44.410
And it says that you need
to break down that wall.

00:03:44.410 --> 00:03:47.590
So DevOps is kind of
an abstract interface.

00:03:47.590 --> 00:03:49.930
It says that it's a set of
practices and guidelines

00:03:49.930 --> 00:03:52.000
that say you should make
sure that you dismantle

00:03:52.000 --> 00:03:54.580
these walls between IT,
operations, software

00:03:54.580 --> 00:03:56.050
development, and so forth.

00:03:56.050 --> 00:03:59.180
But it doesn't say exactly how
do you go about doing that,

00:03:59.180 --> 00:03:59.680
right?

00:03:59.680 --> 00:04:03.370
It leaves a lot of it up to you,
as the person implementing it,

00:04:03.370 --> 00:04:05.820
to figure out what
you want to do.

00:04:05.820 --> 00:04:08.961
In comparison, SRE, or site
reliability engineering,

00:04:08.961 --> 00:04:11.210
it's a set of practices that
were originally developed

00:04:11.210 --> 00:04:13.180
at Google and have spread
across the industry.

00:04:13.180 --> 00:04:15.400
And it's much more prescriptive.

00:04:15.400 --> 00:04:17.790
It says here are some
practices, here are

00:04:17.790 --> 00:04:20.410
some concrete beliefs
and philosophies,

00:04:20.410 --> 00:04:22.360
and here is a job role.

00:04:22.360 --> 00:04:24.100
You'll notice that
as much as people

00:04:24.100 --> 00:04:26.170
like to hire for
DevOps engineers,

00:04:26.170 --> 00:04:28.900
there is no such thing
as a DevOps engineer.

00:04:28.900 --> 00:04:31.690
Whereas you can hire a
site reliability engineer

00:04:31.690 --> 00:04:33.460
and have a relatively
clear idea of what

00:04:33.460 --> 00:04:35.170
does that job description mean.

00:04:35.170 --> 00:04:38.260
So what is in that job
description, right?

00:04:38.260 --> 00:04:40.660
The core idea behind
SRE's solution

00:04:40.660 --> 00:04:43.390
to this problem of the wall
with development and operations

00:04:43.390 --> 00:04:46.850
is that we have to use data
to guide our decision making.

00:04:46.850 --> 00:04:49.930
SRE says that we need
to treat operations just

00:04:49.930 --> 00:04:53.200
like any other part of
software engineering, that we

00:04:53.200 --> 00:04:54.910
need to really
engineer things, right?

00:04:54.910 --> 00:04:58.210
That we need to write automation
to deal with our problems,

00:04:58.210 --> 00:05:01.780
that we need to not be doing
things by hand all the time.

00:05:01.780 --> 00:05:04.780
And that furthermore,
we can't bolt reliably

00:05:04.780 --> 00:05:06.640
on after the fact, that
we have to make sure

00:05:06.640 --> 00:05:08.980
that our systems are
engineered to be reliable

00:05:08.980 --> 00:05:12.070
right from the start.

00:05:12.070 --> 00:05:14.710
In terms of what SRE
teams actually do,

00:05:14.710 --> 00:05:16.645
we are a solution for
having your systems

00:05:16.645 --> 00:05:22.160
run at large scale scalably,
reliably, and efficiently.

00:05:22.160 --> 00:05:24.190
So I don't have
time to go into kind

00:05:24.190 --> 00:05:26.230
of all of these
detailed practices, kind

00:05:26.230 --> 00:05:28.816
of core competencies that
SREs are expected to have.

00:05:28.816 --> 00:05:30.190
For more information
on that, you

00:05:30.190 --> 00:05:32.170
can go to a talk
tomorrow afternoon.

00:05:32.170 --> 00:05:34.030
But I did want to
quickly summarize kind

00:05:34.030 --> 00:05:36.510
of what are the areas, right?

00:05:36.510 --> 00:05:38.440
SREs think about
metrics and monitoring.

00:05:38.440 --> 00:05:40.730
How do we know whether
our systems are working?

00:05:40.730 --> 00:05:42.311
How do we debug them?

00:05:42.311 --> 00:05:43.810
How do we make sure
that our systems

00:05:43.810 --> 00:05:45.476
can scale up appropriately?

00:05:45.476 --> 00:05:47.350
How do we make sure that
we can push software

00:05:47.350 --> 00:05:49.376
to production in a safe manner?

00:05:49.376 --> 00:05:51.250
And when things do break,
how do we make sure

00:05:51.250 --> 00:05:53.140
that we get the system
back within parameters

00:05:53.140 --> 00:05:54.856
in a reasonable amount of time?

00:05:54.856 --> 00:05:56.230
And overarching
all that, we like

00:05:56.230 --> 00:05:57.354
to think about the culture.

00:05:57.354 --> 00:06:00.670
How do I keep operating
the software at scale

00:06:00.670 --> 00:06:03.050
without causing myself
to burn out not today,

00:06:03.050 --> 00:06:05.390
not tomorrow, but a year
from now, two years from now,

00:06:05.390 --> 00:06:06.675
three years from now?

00:06:06.675 --> 00:06:09.050
So that's kind of in general
what we think about as SREs.

00:06:11.387 --> 00:06:13.720
And you may be asking, OK,
so you said a bunch of stuff.

00:06:13.720 --> 00:06:15.590
How does this relate to DevOps?

00:06:15.590 --> 00:06:18.610
So I said earlier that DevOps
was an interface, right?

00:06:18.610 --> 00:06:20.710
That it was an abstract
interface that said here's

00:06:20.710 --> 00:06:21.757
some general ideas.

00:06:21.757 --> 00:06:23.590
It's up to you to figure
out how to do them.

00:06:23.590 --> 00:06:25.870
So these are the
five areas of DevOps.

00:06:25.870 --> 00:06:27.340
DevOps says that
you need to reduce

00:06:27.340 --> 00:06:28.930
your organizational silos.

00:06:28.930 --> 00:06:31.450
It says that we need to have
an idea of failure as normal

00:06:31.450 --> 00:06:33.407
rather than expecting 100%.

00:06:33.407 --> 00:06:35.490
It says that we need to
gradually roll things out,

00:06:35.490 --> 00:06:37.832
that we need to gradually
change or our processes,

00:06:37.832 --> 00:06:39.790
and that we need to use
tooling and automation,

00:06:39.790 --> 00:06:41.350
and that we need
to use measurement

00:06:41.350 --> 00:06:43.900
as the centerpiece
of everything.

00:06:43.900 --> 00:06:46.240
So it turns out the
SRE and DevOps are not

00:06:46.240 --> 00:06:49.317
that far apart from each other
and that every single practice

00:06:49.317 --> 00:06:51.400
that DevOps says you should
have an implementation

00:06:51.400 --> 00:06:53.830
for, SRE does this.

00:06:53.830 --> 00:06:56.890
So today, we're going to tell
you about two specific ways

00:06:56.890 --> 00:06:58.159
that SRE implements DevOps.

00:06:58.159 --> 00:06:59.950
Specifically, we're
going to be telling you

00:06:59.950 --> 00:07:01.790
about error budgets,
and we're going

00:07:01.790 --> 00:07:04.840
to tell you about how to
reduce the cost of failure.

00:07:04.840 --> 00:07:06.869
So hopefully we can go
into a fair bit of depth

00:07:06.869 --> 00:07:08.660
here so that you have
an idea of concretely

00:07:08.660 --> 00:07:10.600
how can Stackdriver,
how can GCP help you

00:07:10.600 --> 00:07:12.580
in each of these two areas.

00:07:12.580 --> 00:07:15.160
So Marie, why don't you
tell us about error budgets?

00:07:15.160 --> 00:07:16.870
MARIA COSGROVE-DAVIES:
All right.

00:07:16.870 --> 00:07:18.670
So I'm going to
talk a little bit

00:07:18.670 --> 00:07:20.870
about error budget and SLOs.

00:07:20.870 --> 00:07:23.140
Now, one of the
core observations

00:07:23.140 --> 00:07:25.000
of site reliability
engineering came

00:07:25.000 --> 00:07:28.300
from Ben Treynor Sloss, who's
the head of all SREs at Google.

00:07:28.300 --> 00:07:32.020
And he noticed that 100% is the
wrong target for everything.

00:07:32.020 --> 00:07:33.760
Like pacemakers, maybe.

00:07:33.760 --> 00:07:34.810
Nuclear missiles, maybe.

00:07:34.810 --> 00:07:36.310
But pretty much
everything else, you

00:07:36.310 --> 00:07:38.650
don't need 100% reliability.

00:07:38.650 --> 00:07:40.330
And why is this?

00:07:40.330 --> 00:07:44.290
If you're running an app, the
network is not 100% reliable.

00:07:44.290 --> 00:07:46.660
Your user's phones
are not 100% reliable.

00:07:46.660 --> 00:07:48.970
They can't tell if it's
your app, or the network,

00:07:48.970 --> 00:07:50.770
or your phones.

00:07:50.770 --> 00:07:53.440
Also, additional
levels of reliability

00:07:53.440 --> 00:07:55.450
require additional expense.

00:07:55.450 --> 00:07:57.910
Google has observed
that for every nine,

00:07:57.910 --> 00:07:58.940
you add reliability.

00:07:58.940 --> 00:08:01.060
And we'll talk about what
that means in a minute.

00:08:01.060 --> 00:08:02.899
You're adding 10x the expense.

00:08:02.899 --> 00:08:04.440
So it gets much,
much more expensive.

00:08:04.440 --> 00:08:05.410
You hire ops people.

00:08:05.410 --> 00:08:07.660
You have to have additional
fallbacks and fail-safes.

00:08:07.660 --> 00:08:09.370
And you have to build for it.

00:08:09.370 --> 00:08:12.730
Since we're not the
airline industry mostly,

00:08:12.730 --> 00:08:15.140
we don't need to.

00:08:15.140 --> 00:08:18.010
So how do we figure this out?

00:08:18.010 --> 00:08:20.260
We use a concept
called error budgets.

00:08:20.260 --> 00:08:22.270
And in order to define
an error budget,

00:08:22.270 --> 00:08:25.660
product management
and SREs will talk

00:08:25.660 --> 00:08:27.760
and will figure out what
we think the users are

00:08:27.760 --> 00:08:30.200
going to be happy with.

00:08:30.200 --> 00:08:34.720
And from that, we subtract
whatever level of availability

00:08:34.720 --> 00:08:36.684
we think the users
need from 100%.

00:08:36.684 --> 00:08:38.350
And the amount that's
left is the amount

00:08:38.350 --> 00:08:40.450
we're allowed to be
down in a given window.

00:08:40.450 --> 00:08:42.970
So that's our error budget.

00:08:42.970 --> 00:08:45.250
Once we have an error
budget, we need to track it.

00:08:45.250 --> 00:08:47.080
It's really important
that we monitor

00:08:47.080 --> 00:08:49.120
the actual availability,
because otherwise,

00:08:49.120 --> 00:08:51.500
we don't know how much of our
budget we've gone through.

00:08:51.500 --> 00:08:53.080
It's like if you have
a budget for spending

00:08:53.080 --> 00:08:54.705
and you don't track
how much you spend,

00:08:54.705 --> 00:08:56.902
then it's not all that useful.

00:08:56.902 --> 00:08:58.360
And when you're
monitoring that, it

00:08:58.360 --> 00:09:01.180
gives you a control loop
for your error budget.

00:09:01.180 --> 00:09:03.730
If you have a ton to spend,
then you can do more releases.

00:09:03.730 --> 00:09:05.230
You can have more features.

00:09:05.230 --> 00:09:06.790
If you run out or
you're running low,

00:09:06.790 --> 00:09:08.380
you need to be
more conservative,

00:09:08.380 --> 00:09:12.090
because you know that your
users are going to be unhappy.

00:09:12.090 --> 00:09:13.580
So how do we
measure reliability?

00:09:13.580 --> 00:09:15.170
What is this metric?

00:09:15.170 --> 00:09:18.450
There's a naive approach,
which is to say, OK, well,

00:09:18.450 --> 00:09:19.999
we can figure out availability.

00:09:19.999 --> 00:09:22.040
That's the number of good
minutes over the number

00:09:22.040 --> 00:09:23.270
of total minutes.

00:09:23.270 --> 00:09:25.799
And that's the amount of
time a service is working.

00:09:25.799 --> 00:09:27.590
There are some advantages
to this approach.

00:09:27.590 --> 00:09:30.410
It's super intuitive,
and it's pretty easy

00:09:30.410 --> 00:09:34.120
to measure for a continuous
binary metric like uptime.

00:09:34.120 --> 00:09:35.960
There is one major
disadvantage, which

00:09:35.960 --> 00:09:37.850
is how many of us
are running services

00:09:37.850 --> 00:09:39.800
that are actually up or down?

00:09:39.800 --> 00:09:44.374
It's really hard for distributed
systems to make sense of this.

00:09:44.374 --> 00:09:46.040
So there's a more
sophisticated approach

00:09:46.040 --> 00:09:47.670
that you may want
to use as well,

00:09:47.670 --> 00:09:49.130
which looks at interactions.

00:09:49.130 --> 00:09:52.490
You take the good interactions
over the total interactions,

00:09:52.490 --> 00:09:54.110
and that's the
fraction of real users

00:09:54.110 --> 00:09:55.820
for whom the system is working.

00:09:55.820 --> 00:09:58.094
Another advantage of this
is it brings the focus back

00:09:58.094 --> 00:10:00.260
to the user, who's really
the person we're concerned

00:10:00.260 --> 00:10:02.104
about in the first place.

00:10:02.104 --> 00:10:03.770
So this handles our
distributor requests

00:10:03.770 --> 00:10:05.360
response services well.

00:10:05.360 --> 00:10:07.790
And it enables cases
like is something that's

00:10:07.790 --> 00:10:10.400
down and not getting requests?

00:10:10.400 --> 00:10:11.730
Do we care?

00:10:11.730 --> 00:10:14.630
No, because we're not
getting any interactions.

00:10:14.630 --> 00:10:16.340
If a few of our
services are down

00:10:16.340 --> 00:10:18.650
and they are getting requests,
what's our availability?

00:10:18.650 --> 00:10:20.000
We look at the interactions.

00:10:20.000 --> 00:10:21.890
There we go.

00:10:21.890 --> 00:10:24.860
This is a hard-to-read chart.

00:10:24.860 --> 00:10:30.930
For me at least, when I think
about 99% or 99.9% or 99.99%,

00:10:30.930 --> 00:10:33.692
it's a little difficult
to keep those straight,

00:10:33.692 --> 00:10:35.150
because they're
all really similar.

00:10:35.150 --> 00:10:37.179
They're all really high numbers.

00:10:37.179 --> 00:10:39.470
It's more useful for me to
think about the error budget

00:10:39.470 --> 00:10:40.570
that I'm allowed.

00:10:40.570 --> 00:10:42.740
Per 30 days, for
instance, if I have

00:10:42.740 --> 00:10:46.850
three nines of availability,
that's 43 minutes of downtime.

00:10:46.850 --> 00:10:50.850
If I have four nines,
that's 4.32 minutes.

00:10:50.850 --> 00:10:52.580
So there is a
dramatic difference

00:10:52.580 --> 00:10:55.850
if I'm operating a system in
how I need to react to an outage

00:10:55.850 --> 00:10:58.550
if I'm expected to have
four nines of availability.

00:10:58.550 --> 00:11:01.133
LIZ FONG-JONES: The other really
interesting thing about this,

00:11:01.133 --> 00:11:03.442
of course, is that we're
assuming 100% down here.

00:11:03.442 --> 00:11:05.900
But earlier, Maria, you were
talking about bad interactions

00:11:05.900 --> 00:11:07.050
and good interactions.

00:11:07.050 --> 00:11:09.440
So if I were serving
10% errors, I

00:11:09.440 --> 00:11:12.890
might still be able to
have 99.99% of my requests

00:11:12.890 --> 00:11:16.730
complete successfully over
30 days for up to 40 minutes

00:11:16.730 --> 00:11:19.880
if I were only 10% down
rather than 100% down.

00:11:19.880 --> 00:11:22.410
So it lets us be a lot more
nuanced about approaching this.

00:11:22.410 --> 00:11:23.993
MARIA COSGROVE-DAVIES:
Yup, but that's

00:11:23.993 --> 00:11:27.950
real hard to represent in a
table, so this is what you get.

00:11:27.950 --> 00:11:30.530
A glossary of terms before
we go into more of this.

00:11:30.530 --> 00:11:33.260
I'm going to be talking
about several of these.

00:11:33.260 --> 00:11:36.110
A service level
indicator, or SLI,

00:11:36.110 --> 00:11:38.177
is a well-defined
metric of good enough.

00:11:38.177 --> 00:11:40.760
And that's what we were talking
about before, your metric that

00:11:40.760 --> 00:11:43.390
helps you figure out
your error budget.

00:11:43.390 --> 00:11:46.580
A service level objective
is a top line target.

00:11:46.580 --> 00:11:49.220
So you combine this
with an SLI and that

00:11:49.220 --> 00:11:51.560
tells you what your goal is.

00:11:51.560 --> 00:11:54.470
And a service level agreement
is a legal agreement

00:11:54.470 --> 00:11:56.100
between you and a user.

00:11:56.100 --> 00:11:59.300
If you fall below your SLA, then
there's some sort of penalty.

00:11:59.300 --> 00:12:01.856
Usually, you will pay the user.

00:12:01.856 --> 00:12:03.980
We won't really be talking
about SLAs in this talk,

00:12:03.980 --> 00:12:06.030
because neither
of us is a lawyer.

00:12:06.030 --> 00:12:08.280
And we think the operations
side is more interesting.

00:12:08.280 --> 00:12:09.821
LIZ FONG-JONES: So
to talk concretely

00:12:09.821 --> 00:12:12.200
briefly about what an SLI
is and what an SLO is,

00:12:12.200 --> 00:12:13.880
you can think about
an SLO as being,

00:12:13.880 --> 00:12:16.940
like, I want this
request to the home page

00:12:16.940 --> 00:12:18.750
to complete in 300 milliseconds.

00:12:18.750 --> 00:12:21.322
If it completes in 300
milliseconds, the SLI is good.

00:12:21.322 --> 00:12:23.405
And if it completes in
more than 300 milliseconds,

00:12:23.405 --> 00:12:24.860
then the SLI has gone sour.

00:12:24.860 --> 00:12:26.430
The users are not quite happy.

00:12:26.430 --> 00:12:28.260
And the SLO is the
top line target

00:12:28.260 --> 00:12:31.460
that says that we expect
99.99, for instance, percent

00:12:31.460 --> 00:12:34.490
of those requests to complete
successfully over a window,

00:12:34.490 --> 00:12:36.690
to make it a little
bit more concrete.

00:12:36.690 --> 00:12:39.080
MARIA COSGROVE-DAVIES:
That's exactly right.

00:12:39.080 --> 00:12:42.590
So how does Stackdriver help
you with SLO monitoring?

00:12:42.590 --> 00:12:46.850
We have an EAP product that
allows you to define, track,

00:12:46.850 --> 00:12:50.091
and alert on SLOs directly
within Stackdriver monitoring.

00:12:50.091 --> 00:12:51.840
I'm just going to walk
you through the UI.

00:12:51.840 --> 00:12:53.392
It's very intuitive.

00:12:53.392 --> 00:12:55.100
The first step is to
figure out if you're

00:12:55.100 --> 00:12:57.950
going to have a latency
or availability SLI.

00:12:57.950 --> 00:13:01.620
If it's latency, you'll define
a number of milliseconds.

00:13:01.620 --> 00:13:03.390
The second is to
look at the period.

00:13:03.390 --> 00:13:07.560
In this case, we're looking
at a week or a month.

00:13:07.560 --> 00:13:10.920
And the third is to look
at your goal or your SLO.

00:13:10.920 --> 00:13:13.600
Now, based, again,
on our research,

00:13:13.600 --> 00:13:17.100
three nines you can get from
well-engineered software.

00:13:17.100 --> 00:13:20.204
Four nines, you can get from
well-engineered operations.

00:13:20.204 --> 00:13:22.620
And five nines, you need a
well-engineered business that's

00:13:22.620 --> 00:13:24.540
really focused on availability.

00:13:24.540 --> 00:13:27.120
And as you'll see from these
very cunning dollar signs

00:13:27.120 --> 00:13:29.370
that we've put on here,
it gets incrementally more

00:13:29.370 --> 00:13:31.440
expensive for
every nine you add.

00:13:31.440 --> 00:13:33.106
LIZ FONG-JONES: So
you may want to think

00:13:33.106 --> 00:13:35.550
about does it really matter
if your users see downtime

00:13:35.550 --> 00:13:37.920
for four seconds per month?

00:13:37.920 --> 00:13:38.796
Maybe, maybe not.

00:13:38.796 --> 00:13:40.420
It's an important
thing to think about.

00:13:40.420 --> 00:13:41.800
What is your specific use case?

00:13:43.624 --> 00:13:45.040
MARIA COSGROVE-DAVIES:
And then we

00:13:45.040 --> 00:13:48.040
have a review, where we
confirm that it looks right.

00:13:48.040 --> 00:13:50.080
We submit, and we
start tracking.

00:13:50.080 --> 00:13:53.065
So once we're tracking our SLO
within Stackdriver monitoring,

00:13:53.065 --> 00:13:54.190
this is what it looks like.

00:13:54.190 --> 00:13:55.570
We get three graphs.

00:13:55.570 --> 00:13:58.360
Availability-- in this
case, in the calendar week.

00:13:58.360 --> 00:14:02.500
And it shows us what
our SLO target is.

00:14:02.500 --> 00:14:06.610
We have our error budget,
and how we're doing.

00:14:06.610 --> 00:14:08.610
And we have our SLO compliance.

00:14:08.610 --> 00:14:11.175
So in this case, we're
not doing so great.

00:14:11.175 --> 00:14:14.664
LIZ FONG-JONES: No,
not doing well at all.

00:14:14.664 --> 00:14:17.080
So now that we've defined or
service level objectives, you

00:14:17.080 --> 00:14:19.937
may ask, that's great, but how
do you actually defend them?

00:14:19.937 --> 00:14:22.270
How do you actually make sure
that you meet your service

00:14:22.270 --> 00:14:23.662
level objectives?

00:14:23.662 --> 00:14:25.120
So let's talk about
the life cycle.

00:14:25.120 --> 00:14:28.240
Let's talk about how do we
manage to run a control loop?

00:14:28.240 --> 00:14:30.009
How do we manage to
keep our services

00:14:30.009 --> 00:14:32.050
running according to the
service level indicators

00:14:32.050 --> 00:14:33.610
and the expectations?

00:14:33.610 --> 00:14:36.100
So typically, your service
level objectives are met.

00:14:36.100 --> 00:14:39.280
Maybe you have 0.5%
errors or 0.001% errors.

00:14:39.280 --> 00:14:42.130
But in general, when your users
are happy, everything is fine.

00:14:42.130 --> 00:14:44.842
But inevitably, faults
do happen in our systems.

00:14:44.842 --> 00:14:47.050
Whether it's a code push,
whether it's a config push,

00:14:47.050 --> 00:14:49.810
or a surge of the user
traffic, something

00:14:49.810 --> 00:14:52.380
will happen inside of your
system that is a fault.

00:14:52.380 --> 00:14:54.960
At that point, what happens
is your service level

00:14:54.960 --> 00:14:57.040
indicators start going
sour, because users

00:14:57.040 --> 00:14:58.600
are having a bad experience.

00:14:58.600 --> 00:15:00.850
And Stackdriver SLO
might eventually

00:15:00.850 --> 00:15:03.370
let you know that your
error budget is in danger.

00:15:03.370 --> 00:15:06.100
So your alerting
might fire, and then

00:15:06.100 --> 00:15:08.530
we have to do things as the
people who are responding,

00:15:08.530 --> 00:15:09.030
right?

00:15:09.030 --> 00:15:11.985
We have to see is everything
100% down globally?

00:15:11.985 --> 00:15:14.121
If so, I might want to
involve more people.

00:15:14.121 --> 00:15:16.120
Can I do something quickly
like draining a zone?

00:15:16.120 --> 00:15:17.694
If so, maybe I'll do that.

00:15:17.694 --> 00:15:19.360
But then after that,
we wind up spending

00:15:19.360 --> 00:15:22.720
a lot of time, in the course
of figuring out what happened,

00:15:22.720 --> 00:15:23.470
in this loop.

00:15:23.470 --> 00:15:26.140
We wind up having to
formulate a hypothesis of what

00:15:26.140 --> 00:15:28.690
we think broken our system.

00:15:28.690 --> 00:15:30.760
And then we wind up having
to test the hypothesis

00:15:30.760 --> 00:15:34.060
and actually verify is this
actually what is going on

00:15:34.060 --> 00:15:37.180
in my system or did I come to
the wrong conclusion about what

00:15:37.180 --> 00:15:38.104
is actually broken?

00:15:38.104 --> 00:15:40.270
And assuming that you've
identified the right thing,

00:15:40.270 --> 00:15:41.800
then you go and develop a fix.

00:15:41.800 --> 00:15:43.960
Maybe it's provisioning
more capacity.

00:15:43.960 --> 00:15:45.610
Maybe it is reverting
the bad rollout.

00:15:45.610 --> 00:15:48.570
But you have to develop a fix,
and you have to test the fix.

00:15:48.570 --> 00:15:51.190
And ideally, everything
goes back to normal, right?

00:15:51.190 --> 00:15:53.050
Everything goes back
to nominal conditions,

00:15:53.050 --> 00:15:55.720
and your SLO is met again.

00:15:55.720 --> 00:15:58.360
Unfortunately, how
many of you have ever

00:15:58.360 --> 00:16:01.405
had a situation where your
first guess was right about what

00:16:01.405 --> 00:16:02.530
broke in the system, right?

00:16:02.530 --> 00:16:05.950
How many of you-- it
happens every single time?

00:16:05.950 --> 00:16:07.420
No one's raising their hands.

00:16:07.420 --> 00:16:09.610
Good, none of you are
overconfident, right?

00:16:09.610 --> 00:16:13.420
We spend a lot of time, because
our hypotheses are not always

00:16:13.420 --> 00:16:16.900
correct, or our solutions are
not always correct, right?

00:16:16.900 --> 00:16:19.990
So we wind up going back
to square one over and over

00:16:19.990 --> 00:16:21.110
and over again.

00:16:21.110 --> 00:16:22.870
So it's really, really
important for us

00:16:22.870 --> 00:16:25.690
to both have high quality
alerting to make sure

00:16:25.690 --> 00:16:29.350
that we find out quickly but
without waking us up too much,

00:16:29.350 --> 00:16:31.930
and then we need to make
sure that we're actually

00:16:31.930 --> 00:16:34.060
getting to testing and
formulating hypotheses

00:16:34.060 --> 00:16:36.080
relatively quickly.

00:16:36.080 --> 00:16:38.502
So let's start with the high
accuracy alerting bit, right?

00:16:38.502 --> 00:16:40.960
We want to make sure that we
don't burn out, that we're not

00:16:40.960 --> 00:16:43.460
getting paged 30 times in the
middle of the night at 2:00 AM

00:16:43.460 --> 00:16:45.292
every single day.

00:16:45.292 --> 00:16:46.750
At the end of the
day, what matters

00:16:46.750 --> 00:16:49.210
is that we have to monitor
the symptoms of what

00:16:49.210 --> 00:16:52.150
our users are actually seeing
and not every potential root

00:16:52.150 --> 00:16:52.650
cause.

00:16:52.650 --> 00:16:53.610
What do I mean by this?

00:16:53.610 --> 00:16:56.290
Well, so users care if
they can use our service.

00:16:56.290 --> 00:16:58.600
And getting alerted about
things that don't actually

00:16:58.600 --> 00:17:01.300
impact your users doesn't
really matter, right?

00:17:01.300 --> 00:17:04.300
Does it really impact your
users if one of your VMs

00:17:04.300 --> 00:17:06.040
has its hard disk fill up?

00:17:06.040 --> 00:17:07.627
Does it really matter?

00:17:07.627 --> 00:17:09.460
Probably not, but it
kind of depends on what

00:17:09.460 --> 00:17:11.380
your users are experiencing.

00:17:11.380 --> 00:17:14.030
So what about if your
paxos quorum changes?

00:17:14.030 --> 00:17:16.569
Or what about if the seat
you load on one machine

00:17:16.569 --> 00:17:17.210
is too high?

00:17:17.210 --> 00:17:21.079
Or what if the queue length
on your [INAUDIBLE] queue

00:17:21.079 --> 00:17:22.150
is too long?

00:17:22.150 --> 00:17:23.500
Or what if your task restarts?

00:17:23.500 --> 00:17:25.280
Or what if the
kernel panics, right?

00:17:25.280 --> 00:17:27.640
Like, all of these possible
things that could possibly

00:17:27.640 --> 00:17:32.920
go wrong, not every single one
of them is worth waking up for.

00:17:32.920 --> 00:17:35.180
So it turns out that
there's a better way

00:17:35.180 --> 00:17:36.430
of thinking about this, right?

00:17:36.430 --> 00:17:39.310
Because if we're already
measuring your service level

00:17:39.310 --> 00:17:41.320
objectives, your
service level objectives

00:17:41.320 --> 00:17:43.540
are measuring user happiness.

00:17:43.540 --> 00:17:46.210
So what you care
about is figuring out

00:17:46.210 --> 00:17:48.130
is your user happiness impacted?

00:17:48.130 --> 00:17:51.310
For instance, if I had
an availability SLI that

00:17:51.310 --> 00:17:54.370
said this is the
fraction of HTTP 500s

00:17:54.370 --> 00:17:56.440
that I'm willing to
serve, and any more

00:17:56.440 --> 00:17:59.830
than that is not acceptable,
that is an end user symptom.

00:17:59.830 --> 00:18:02.360
How many 500s am I serving?

00:18:02.360 --> 00:18:03.790
And I can use that
to my advantage

00:18:03.790 --> 00:18:07.450
to replace all of those other
alerts that I showed you

00:18:07.450 --> 00:18:10.000
and get rid of them, because
all I care about is, are

00:18:10.000 --> 00:18:13.527
my users seeing errors or not?

00:18:13.527 --> 00:18:15.610
But there also has to be
another strategy to this,

00:18:15.610 --> 00:18:17.590
because we need to make
sure that we don't have

00:18:17.590 --> 00:18:19.460
too many false alarms, right?

00:18:19.460 --> 00:18:22.630
But that we also detect
low level degradations

00:18:22.630 --> 00:18:24.470
in our systems.

00:18:24.470 --> 00:18:26.800
So we have to go back to
our service level objective

00:18:26.800 --> 00:18:30.250
and say, what's an
acceptable burn rate?

00:18:30.250 --> 00:18:33.550
And how will I know quickly
enough to react in the event

00:18:33.550 --> 00:18:35.680
that I'm going to consume
weeks of my error budget

00:18:35.680 --> 00:18:37.990
in a matter of hours
or minutes, right?

00:18:37.990 --> 00:18:41.890
As well as being able to
keep track of am I having 10%

00:18:41.890 --> 00:18:44.200
more errors than I am allowed?

00:18:44.200 --> 00:18:48.400
Am I getting instead of
0.1% errors, 0.11% errors

00:18:48.400 --> 00:18:50.554
over a longer window?

00:18:50.554 --> 00:18:51.970
So this means that
I need to think

00:18:51.970 --> 00:18:54.580
about what are my thresholds.

00:18:54.580 --> 00:18:55.930
I need to think about the math.

00:18:55.930 --> 00:18:58.390
I need to say, if
I'm overspending,

00:18:58.390 --> 00:19:02.180
and I'm spending five hours of
error budget in a single hour,

00:19:02.180 --> 00:19:03.600
I should probably
fire off a page.

00:19:03.600 --> 00:19:05.350
I should probably wake
someone up and have

00:19:05.350 --> 00:19:07.380
them immediately deal with it.

00:19:07.380 --> 00:19:11.980
Or if I've overspent by
5% or 10% over a few days,

00:19:11.980 --> 00:19:14.050
it's been going
on for a few days.

00:19:14.050 --> 00:19:15.687
It will probably
take multiple weeks

00:19:15.687 --> 00:19:17.020
to burn through my error budget.

00:19:17.020 --> 00:19:18.530
It can wait until the
next business day,

00:19:18.530 --> 00:19:19.988
it can wait for
someone to look at.

00:19:19.988 --> 00:19:22.600
It's still urgent, but not
waking-someone-up urgent.

00:19:22.600 --> 00:19:24.160
And that way, you
get enough time

00:19:24.160 --> 00:19:25.900
to get that statistical
signal of is

00:19:25.900 --> 00:19:28.204
something actually broken.

00:19:28.204 --> 00:19:29.620
So essentially,
what we've done is

00:19:29.620 --> 00:19:33.100
we said, if we overspend by
too much within a given window,

00:19:33.100 --> 00:19:36.130
for instance, one hour, that
we need to fire that page.

00:19:36.130 --> 00:19:39.490
But also, if we burn faster
than five or 10 times

00:19:39.490 --> 00:19:42.440
the rate that I'm allowed
in my budget-- for instance,

00:19:42.440 --> 00:19:44.950
if I wind up burning
through seven

00:19:44.950 --> 00:19:46.832
hours of error budget
in five minutes,

00:19:46.832 --> 00:19:48.040
I'll get notified right away.

00:19:48.040 --> 00:19:51.399
There's no need to
wait the full hour.

00:19:51.399 --> 00:19:53.690
Similarly, we can talk about
the slow burn case, right?

00:19:53.690 --> 00:19:57.130
We can say, OK, I'm going
to measure over three days,

00:19:57.130 --> 00:20:00.370
and if I see 5% more
errors than I expect,

00:20:00.370 --> 00:20:02.230
then I'm going to file a ticket.

00:20:02.230 --> 00:20:04.930
I'm going to get a Jira
ticket or whatever, right?

00:20:04.930 --> 00:20:08.570
Or if I even cross that
threshold within one day,

00:20:08.570 --> 00:20:10.490
I can still get a
ticket and deal with it,

00:20:10.490 --> 00:20:12.760
and it'll be fine.

00:20:12.760 --> 00:20:14.800
So the good news
is you don't have

00:20:14.800 --> 00:20:16.540
to implement all of
that math yourself,

00:20:16.540 --> 00:20:20.880
because Stackdriver SLO will
deal with that problem for you.

00:20:20.880 --> 00:20:24.280
Stackdriver SLO will let you
send alerts when your error

00:20:24.280 --> 00:20:25.990
budget starts being exhausted.

00:20:25.990 --> 00:20:27.880
It will project out
into the future,

00:20:27.880 --> 00:20:30.550
and it will say, if I'm
going to exhaust my error

00:20:30.550 --> 00:20:33.517
budget within a
certain window of time,

00:20:33.517 --> 00:20:34.600
then I need to be alerted.

00:20:34.600 --> 00:20:36.730
Or if I have less than
10% of my error budget

00:20:36.730 --> 00:20:38.860
for the month remaining
or for the week remaining,

00:20:38.860 --> 00:20:40.060
to alert me.

00:20:40.060 --> 00:20:43.090
And that is how you get rid
of all of those potential

00:20:43.090 --> 00:20:45.820
cause-based alerts and
instead replace them with

00:20:45.820 --> 00:20:49.850
service-level-objective-based
monitoring.

00:20:49.850 --> 00:20:51.100
So that's great, right?

00:20:51.100 --> 00:20:54.250
We've now addressed the
time-to-detect problem.

00:20:54.250 --> 00:20:57.070
But let's talk also about
the time-to-resolve problem,

00:20:57.070 --> 00:20:59.530
because that's also an
equally important part

00:20:59.530 --> 00:21:01.640
of having a resilient service.

00:21:01.640 --> 00:21:02.140
So

00:21:02.140 --> 00:21:05.530
When users see errors, now
we have our service level

00:21:05.530 --> 00:21:07.020
indicators going sour.

00:21:07.020 --> 00:21:08.890
Stackdriver SLO alerts
us, because we're

00:21:08.890 --> 00:21:10.940
burning through our
error budget too fast.

00:21:10.940 --> 00:21:11.922
What do we need to do?

00:21:11.922 --> 00:21:13.630
As I said earlier in
the debugging cycle,

00:21:13.630 --> 00:21:17.300
we need to prioritize speed
of our mitigation recovery.

00:21:17.300 --> 00:21:19.880
So this is where a technique
called layer peeling comes

00:21:19.880 --> 00:21:21.340
into good effect.

00:21:21.340 --> 00:21:25.210
And it helps us localize
problems within our stack.

00:21:25.210 --> 00:21:27.190
What zone is it coming from?

00:21:27.190 --> 00:21:29.950
Which Kubernetes pot is
it coming from, right?

00:21:29.950 --> 00:21:33.410
These are useful questions that
we need to be able to answer.

00:21:33.410 --> 00:21:36.790
So I'm showing you
how Google SREs debug

00:21:36.790 --> 00:21:39.670
tooling using our internal
infrastructure, which has then

00:21:39.670 --> 00:21:41.920
influenced Stackdriver
to develop features

00:21:41.920 --> 00:21:44.170
that help everyone do
it rather than requiring

00:21:44.170 --> 00:21:46.090
you to be a Google SRE.

00:21:46.090 --> 00:21:47.205
So in this example--

00:21:47.205 --> 00:21:48.580
for instance,
let's say that I've

00:21:48.580 --> 00:21:51.370
been paged for a latency
service level indicator, right?

00:21:51.370 --> 00:21:55.060
That I've started seeing that
my 95th percentile latency

00:21:55.060 --> 00:21:57.680
has gone over 100 milliseconds.

00:21:57.680 --> 00:22:00.550
So I might need to do something
like ask a question, like,

00:22:00.550 --> 00:22:05.540
hey can you break this down for
me by zone within the region?

00:22:05.540 --> 00:22:08.090
So if I do that, then I might
get useful data that says,

00:22:08.090 --> 00:22:11.696
hey, US West 1b has
been slow for a while,

00:22:11.696 --> 00:22:13.570
but that's not where
the excursion came from.

00:22:13.570 --> 00:22:16.030
The excursion came
from US West 1a.

00:22:16.030 --> 00:22:18.070
And then I can drill
down into US West 1a,

00:22:18.070 --> 00:22:21.760
and I can see that it's
two specific replicas that

00:22:21.760 --> 00:22:23.400
are slow.

00:22:23.400 --> 00:22:25.900
And then I can even do something
more sophisticated and say,

00:22:25.900 --> 00:22:26.740
you know what?

00:22:26.740 --> 00:22:28.330
Show me a heat map, right?

00:22:28.330 --> 00:22:32.230
To show me the details of what's
the distribution of requests

00:22:32.230 --> 00:22:33.870
that are slow.

00:22:33.870 --> 00:22:37.100
And then we'll give me a
signal of, for instance,

00:22:37.100 --> 00:22:40.960
all of the queries on this task
are slow because of the fact

00:22:40.960 --> 00:22:42.340
that there is a noisy neighbor.

00:22:42.340 --> 00:22:44.680
And then I might drain it
away from that machine, right?

00:22:44.680 --> 00:22:47.560
So it provides you with an
actionable way of drilling down

00:22:47.560 --> 00:22:49.780
into your data.

00:22:49.780 --> 00:22:52.270
And the good news is that this
is fully implemented inside

00:22:52.270 --> 00:22:54.970
of Stackdriver instead
of the Metrics Explorer.

00:22:54.970 --> 00:22:56.920
So you can go into
the Metrics Explorer,

00:22:56.920 --> 00:22:59.740
and you can ask questions,
and you can tell it to filter,

00:22:59.740 --> 00:23:02.730
and then you can tell it to
group by a specific thing.

00:23:02.730 --> 00:23:04.390
So in the earlier
example, I said

00:23:04.390 --> 00:23:08.950
I wanted to filter by
region and group by zone.

00:23:08.950 --> 00:23:11.860
And then I wanted to
filter by zone and group

00:23:11.860 --> 00:23:13.210
by pod, for instance.

00:23:13.210 --> 00:23:15.100
And then filter by
pod, and then group

00:23:15.100 --> 00:23:17.230
by individual task number.

00:23:17.230 --> 00:23:19.030
So that's how we
think about being

00:23:19.030 --> 00:23:22.780
able to apply this SRE
technique from Google

00:23:22.780 --> 00:23:25.005
inside of Stackdriver.

00:23:25.005 --> 00:23:26.830
So Stackdriver
Metrics Explorer is

00:23:26.830 --> 00:23:28.600
really handy for doing
these drill downs,

00:23:28.600 --> 00:23:31.780
because if you can't localize
problems within your stack,

00:23:31.780 --> 00:23:33.460
you're taking random
guesses, right?

00:23:33.460 --> 00:23:35.920
You're kind of playing
whack-a-mole, seeing

00:23:35.920 --> 00:23:38.050
whether your latest
hypothesis actually

00:23:38.050 --> 00:23:39.199
is where the problem is.

00:23:39.199 --> 00:23:40.240
It's better to not guess.

00:23:40.240 --> 00:23:43.870
It's better to know by
exploring your data by the hag's

00:23:43.870 --> 00:23:46.240
of where it came from

00:23:46.240 --> 00:23:49.160
the second technique I'm going
to tell you about with regard

00:23:49.160 --> 00:23:51.760
to helping you with
debugging problems faster

00:23:51.760 --> 00:23:53.200
and reducing your
time to recover

00:23:53.200 --> 00:23:56.170
is joining to be able to
cross-correlate problems inside

00:23:56.170 --> 00:23:57.457
of your stack.

00:23:57.457 --> 00:23:59.290
What do I mean by cross
correlating problems

00:23:59.290 --> 00:24:00.640
in your stack?

00:24:00.640 --> 00:24:03.340
Well, often, it turns
out to be the case

00:24:03.340 --> 00:24:05.800
that we have pushed
something not

00:24:05.800 --> 00:24:10.230
to any one individual Kubernetes
pod or any individual zone

00:24:10.230 --> 00:24:12.370
or region, but
instead that something

00:24:12.370 --> 00:24:13.810
has happened under the hood.

00:24:13.810 --> 00:24:17.170
For instance, maybe someone
went and silently updated

00:24:17.170 --> 00:24:21.820
the Java virtual machine
across our entire fleet, right?

00:24:21.820 --> 00:24:24.310
So if you look at
all of the data,

00:24:24.310 --> 00:24:27.049
you might just see noise
because of the fact

00:24:27.049 --> 00:24:28.840
that there's no one
particular place that's

00:24:28.840 --> 00:24:31.820
coming from some tasks
are slower than others.

00:24:31.820 --> 00:24:34.390
So how do we get useful
signal out of this?

00:24:34.390 --> 00:24:36.430
What we have to be
able to do is we

00:24:36.430 --> 00:24:40.480
have to be able to combine
latency data with other data,

00:24:40.480 --> 00:24:42.340
such as what version
of the kernel

00:24:42.340 --> 00:24:44.290
we're running, for instance.

00:24:44.290 --> 00:24:46.870
So we do this all
the time at Google,

00:24:46.870 --> 00:24:49.000
where we add additional
information that

00:24:49.000 --> 00:24:50.740
is collected elsewhere
in the system

00:24:50.740 --> 00:24:53.210
and combine it
with existing data.

00:24:53.210 --> 00:24:55.780
And once we have
that additional data,

00:24:55.780 --> 00:24:59.710
we can go ahead and say, hey,
I want the average latency

00:24:59.710 --> 00:25:01.310
by kernel version.

00:25:01.310 --> 00:25:04.240
And if I do that, I get
signal out of my noise.

00:25:04.240 --> 00:25:08.140
And that signal tells me, hey,
that kernel version suddenly

00:25:08.140 --> 00:25:10.120
started having a
regression, where

00:25:10.120 --> 00:25:13.370
previously all I saw was noise.

00:25:13.370 --> 00:25:16.450
So how can you do this inside
of Stackdriver, you may ask?

00:25:16.450 --> 00:25:19.010
The answer is that
inside of the Metrics

00:25:19.010 --> 00:25:21.940
Explorer, any other
view within Stackdriver,

00:25:21.940 --> 00:25:25.960
you can select not only
location-based information,

00:25:25.960 --> 00:25:29.411
but you can also select
for system properties that

00:25:29.411 --> 00:25:31.660
are embedded inside of the
data that you're exporting.

00:25:31.660 --> 00:25:34.810
For instance, you can export
the container image ID out

00:25:34.810 --> 00:25:37.000
of your Kubernetes
cluster in order

00:25:37.000 --> 00:25:40.000
to get a delta between
two different values.

00:25:40.000 --> 00:25:42.760
For instance, in
this example, we

00:25:42.760 --> 00:25:48.010
have set up a Kubernetes pod
to serve 500s at a very--

00:25:48.010 --> 00:25:50.440
to serve 500s very quickly,
because it's not taking

00:25:50.440 --> 00:25:52.390
any time to do any real work.

00:25:52.390 --> 00:25:54.700
And then meanwhile,
the control group

00:25:54.700 --> 00:25:57.490
is serving a regular workload
and taking a normal amount

00:25:57.490 --> 00:25:58.610
of time to do it.

00:25:58.610 --> 00:26:00.610
So being able to do this
breakdown by version,

00:26:00.610 --> 00:26:03.430
not necessarily localized to
any one particular location,

00:26:03.430 --> 00:26:05.870
is really, really helpful.

00:26:05.870 --> 00:26:07.870
The other thing
that's really cool

00:26:07.870 --> 00:26:09.940
is that we have
upcoming a feature that

00:26:09.940 --> 00:26:12.970
will allow you to issue
custom queries that are not

00:26:12.970 --> 00:26:15.280
done through the
UI and are instead

00:26:15.280 --> 00:26:17.780
done by typing in expressions.

00:26:17.780 --> 00:26:20.380
So this lets you combine data
that you wouldn't ordinarily

00:26:20.380 --> 00:26:21.190
be able to combine.

00:26:21.190 --> 00:26:23.920
For instance, being
able to calculate

00:26:23.920 --> 00:26:26.380
how many CPU seconds
does each query

00:26:26.380 --> 00:26:29.810
take that my system
is receiving, right?

00:26:29.810 --> 00:26:33.070
do you have to take the
total number of CPU seconds

00:26:33.070 --> 00:26:36.730
on each task and divide it by
the total number of queries

00:26:36.730 --> 00:26:39.520
on each task, which are metrics
that you wouldn't ordinarily

00:26:39.520 --> 00:26:42.100
be able to combine.

00:26:42.100 --> 00:26:44.620
So this core idea that I'm
trying to get across to you

00:26:44.620 --> 00:26:47.680
is that having your
metadata available to you

00:26:47.680 --> 00:26:51.280
inside of Stackdriver
monitoring and having

00:26:51.280 --> 00:26:53.680
the query notation
language available to you

00:26:53.680 --> 00:26:56.350
is a critical tool for being
able to formulate and test

00:26:56.350 --> 00:26:57.650
your hypotheses.

00:26:57.650 --> 00:27:00.490
And it lets you find problems
that are not necessarily tied

00:27:00.490 --> 00:27:02.450
to a specific location.

00:27:02.450 --> 00:27:06.610
So you can today do
resource-metadata-based queries

00:27:06.610 --> 00:27:09.287
easily within the generally
available product.

00:27:09.287 --> 00:27:10.870
And we have Stackdriver
query notation

00:27:10.870 --> 00:27:13.990
that's in preview that will
allow you to arbitrarily join

00:27:13.990 --> 00:27:15.000
these metrics together.

00:27:15.000 --> 00:27:17.140
And if you're interested in
learning more or putting this

00:27:17.140 --> 00:27:19.420
to work, feel free to talk
to me afterwards or come

00:27:19.420 --> 00:27:23.326
to the Fifth Nine Lounge on the
second floor of Moscone West.

00:27:23.326 --> 00:27:24.700
The final technique
that I wanted

00:27:24.700 --> 00:27:26.380
to show you for reducing
your time to resolve

00:27:26.380 --> 00:27:27.640
is really, really powerful.

00:27:27.640 --> 00:27:30.220
It has seriously changed
my life as an SRE.

00:27:30.220 --> 00:27:32.290
And the technique
is called exemplars.

00:27:32.290 --> 00:27:34.930
It lets you pinpoint problems
within your stack by letting

00:27:34.930 --> 00:27:37.950
you reason starting
from specific examples.

00:27:37.950 --> 00:27:40.070
What do I mean by this?

00:27:40.070 --> 00:27:42.640
An exemplar is
essentially an association

00:27:42.640 --> 00:27:46.240
of something high cardinality
with your existing

00:27:46.240 --> 00:27:48.590
distribution-based data.

00:27:48.590 --> 00:27:51.320
So it makes your
distributed traces really,

00:27:51.320 --> 00:27:53.590
really useful and
actionable for you.

00:27:53.590 --> 00:27:56.420
It makes your high
cardinality data tags really,

00:27:56.420 --> 00:27:58.660
really useful and
actionable to you.

00:27:58.660 --> 00:28:01.870
The way that it works is that
we keep inside of distributions

00:28:01.870 --> 00:28:04.390
the notion of a time
slice in our bucket--

00:28:04.390 --> 00:28:08.660
for instance, the window
between 2:50 PM and 2:55 PM--

00:28:08.660 --> 00:28:11.770
and a value slice, which
is, for instance, the values

00:28:11.770 --> 00:28:14.480
between 50 and 60 milliseconds.

00:28:14.480 --> 00:28:17.620
So for each of those
buckets, we record

00:28:17.620 --> 00:28:22.250
up to one example of a query
exhibiting those behaviors.

00:28:22.250 --> 00:28:24.610
For instance, a
Stackdriver trace

00:28:24.610 --> 00:28:28.420
that exhibits that latency
during that time window.

00:28:28.420 --> 00:28:31.240
And then we can show you in
Stackdriver monitoring which

00:28:31.240 --> 00:28:33.130
buckets have samples
and let you go look

00:28:33.130 --> 00:28:35.320
at the samples in detail.

00:28:35.320 --> 00:28:36.790
So as I said, this
is a technique

00:28:36.790 --> 00:28:38.410
inspired by Google SREs.

00:28:38.410 --> 00:28:40.766
We've been using this
for several years now.

00:28:40.766 --> 00:28:42.640
And what do we actually
use it for at Google?

00:28:42.640 --> 00:28:46.420
The answer is that we use it for
looking at long-tail problems.

00:28:46.420 --> 00:28:49.870
For instance, it would be
prohibitively expensive for us

00:28:49.870 --> 00:28:51.790
to record the
amount of time that

00:28:51.790 --> 00:28:55.690
every single physical machine
in Google's fleet took to boot.

00:28:55.690 --> 00:29:00.220
However, what I can do is I
can record only one example

00:29:00.220 --> 00:29:03.910
for each of these
common case time values.

00:29:03.910 --> 00:29:06.100
But I can also
record one example

00:29:06.100 --> 00:29:07.840
for each of these
outlying values,

00:29:07.840 --> 00:29:10.210
and I can find out,
as a person who

00:29:10.210 --> 00:29:13.120
maintains the fleet of machines,
which specific machine should I

00:29:13.120 --> 00:29:16.810
be looking for that
had a slow reboot time.

00:29:16.810 --> 00:29:18.700
Or for instance,
another thing that I

00:29:18.700 --> 00:29:22.480
can look at is if I have a
service level indicator that

00:29:22.480 --> 00:29:25.910
is based off of a blackbox
prober-- for instance,

00:29:25.910 --> 00:29:28.790
if I had a storage system,
where I was measuring

00:29:28.790 --> 00:29:33.590
how quickly does it take to
write a query into the system,

00:29:33.590 --> 00:29:37.630
what I might observe is if I had
a less sophisticated monitoring

00:29:37.630 --> 00:29:41.440
system, all I would see is that
the 99th percentile is slowly

00:29:41.440 --> 00:29:43.400
creeping up over time.

00:29:43.400 --> 00:29:45.730
However, the cool thing is
that this heat map gives me

00:29:45.730 --> 00:29:47.620
a visual representation
of the fact

00:29:47.620 --> 00:29:50.080
that more and more
of these queries

00:29:50.080 --> 00:29:53.870
are taking close to
N100 milliseconds.

00:29:53.870 --> 00:29:56.950
But there's not really that
many queries over that value.

00:29:56.950 --> 00:29:59.710
So I might ask
questions, like show me

00:29:59.710 --> 00:30:02.800
an example of a query that
takes N100 milliseconds, right?

00:30:02.800 --> 00:30:04.930
Show me an example of a
query somewhere in here

00:30:04.930 --> 00:30:07.660
that is starting to
become very slow.

00:30:07.660 --> 00:30:10.120
And I can then click on
it and find a Stackdriver

00:30:10.120 --> 00:30:13.720
trace corresponding
to that slow event

00:30:13.720 --> 00:30:16.360
so that I can start looking
at this long-tail problem

00:30:16.360 --> 00:30:18.400
rather than having to
look through 20 or 30

00:30:18.400 --> 00:30:22.910
different dashboards and guess
what I think is happening.

00:30:22.910 --> 00:30:25.550
So this feature is going to be
available soon in Stackdriver

00:30:25.550 --> 00:30:30.490
that lets you overlay individual
Stackdriver traces onto a heat

00:30:30.490 --> 00:30:32.530
map of your latencies
and display it

00:30:32.530 --> 00:30:34.870
alongside your service
level objectives.

00:30:34.870 --> 00:30:37.660
So you can go directly
from having a service level

00:30:37.660 --> 00:30:41.280
objective threatened to seen
concrete examples of queries

00:30:41.280 --> 00:30:43.780
that caused your service level
indicators to become unhappy,

00:30:43.780 --> 00:30:46.510
so that you can start debugging
and formulating hypotheses

00:30:46.510 --> 00:30:48.460
faster.

00:30:48.460 --> 00:30:51.010
With these culprit
traces in hand,

00:30:51.010 --> 00:30:52.660
it helps you formulate
a hypothesis.

00:30:52.660 --> 00:30:56.230
It helps you figure out,
which task do I need to go to?

00:30:56.230 --> 00:30:59.020
Which Kubernetes pod
do I need to look at?

00:30:59.020 --> 00:31:01.422
In order to figure
out which logs do I

00:31:01.422 --> 00:31:02.380
need to look at, right?

00:31:02.380 --> 00:31:05.200
Because it has a concrete time
stamp and machine name to go

00:31:05.200 --> 00:31:06.370
look at.

00:31:06.370 --> 00:31:08.650
You still need to be able
to dynamically evaluate

00:31:08.650 --> 00:31:11.770
queries, which is where the
earlier section on joining data

00:31:11.770 --> 00:31:12.850
comes from.

00:31:12.850 --> 00:31:14.920
But even formulating
the hypothesis

00:31:14.920 --> 00:31:17.750
is the thing that we find
we need to optimize for.

00:31:17.750 --> 00:31:19.690
So exemplars and
Stackdriver are a way

00:31:19.690 --> 00:31:22.720
of combining Stackdriver trace
and Stackdriver monitoring

00:31:22.720 --> 00:31:27.990
all in one view and getting the
insights that you need faster.

00:31:27.990 --> 00:31:30.350
So now you've
identified your problem

00:31:30.350 --> 00:31:31.950
and you think it
might be Google.

00:31:31.950 --> 00:31:33.590
What should I do, Marie?

00:31:33.590 --> 00:31:35.256
MARIA COSGROVE-DAVIES:
So Liz talked you

00:31:35.256 --> 00:31:37.520
through some techniques that
Google SREs use in order

00:31:37.520 --> 00:31:39.302
to debug more effectively.

00:31:39.302 --> 00:31:40.760
And I'm going to
show you something

00:31:40.760 --> 00:31:42.890
that Google is providing
to you right now,

00:31:42.890 --> 00:31:45.560
today, that will help
you easily identify

00:31:45.560 --> 00:31:49.469
whether a problem that you've
seen is because of Google.

00:31:49.469 --> 00:31:52.010
Now often, the first thing that
we'll do when we're debugging

00:31:52.010 --> 00:31:53.750
is to try to figure
out roughly where

00:31:53.750 --> 00:31:55.350
the problem's coming from.

00:31:55.350 --> 00:31:56.630
Is it my code?

00:31:56.630 --> 00:31:57.800
Is it the network?

00:31:57.800 --> 00:31:59.390
Is it the infrastructure?

00:31:59.390 --> 00:32:01.820
That can be a really
tricky thing to figure out,

00:32:01.820 --> 00:32:04.040
so Google's
transparent SLIs help

00:32:04.040 --> 00:32:06.980
you answer the question
of is this problem coming

00:32:06.980 --> 00:32:08.810
from the infrastructure.

00:32:08.810 --> 00:32:10.790
They give you the ability
to quickly understand

00:32:10.790 --> 00:32:12.470
if an issue you're
seeing is correlated

00:32:12.470 --> 00:32:15.080
with an issue in GCP.

00:32:15.080 --> 00:32:17.270
And they give you
per project metrics

00:32:17.270 --> 00:32:21.890
about your requests to help
you answer this question.

00:32:21.890 --> 00:32:23.150
How might you use them?

00:32:23.150 --> 00:32:24.530
For triage.

00:32:24.530 --> 00:32:27.560
If you've gotten alerted
by one of your SLIs,

00:32:27.560 --> 00:32:30.320
it's trending towards
violating your error budget.

00:32:30.320 --> 00:32:34.070
This gives you a single place to
go to look at the GCP services

00:32:34.070 --> 00:32:37.020
that your product depends
on, so you can tell.

00:32:37.020 --> 00:32:38.180
And then for support.

00:32:38.180 --> 00:32:41.150
If you do identify an issue,
when you call Google Support,

00:32:41.150 --> 00:32:43.280
you'll be looking
at the same graph.

00:32:43.280 --> 00:32:45.020
And that helps
make sure that you

00:32:45.020 --> 00:32:47.210
start the conversation
on the right foot,

00:32:47.210 --> 00:32:50.210
that you start the conversation
with a mutual understanding

00:32:50.210 --> 00:32:54.810
of the problem, so you can move
quickly towards resolution.

00:32:54.810 --> 00:32:56.640
So to view these--

00:32:56.640 --> 00:33:00.060
again, these are your requests,
so they're per project.

00:33:00.060 --> 00:33:03.780
And they're broken up
by a number of fields.

00:33:03.780 --> 00:33:05.330
In the Metrics Explorer today--

00:33:05.330 --> 00:33:06.830
you could go look
at this right now,

00:33:06.830 --> 00:33:08.880
but please keep paying
attention to our talk.

00:33:08.880 --> 00:33:10.020
Wait until after.

00:33:10.020 --> 00:33:11.982
You'll search for consumed API.

00:33:11.982 --> 00:33:14.190
And when you do this, you'll
see there four options--

00:33:14.190 --> 00:33:16.890
request count, request
latency, request size,

00:33:16.890 --> 00:33:19.619
and response size.

00:33:19.619 --> 00:33:21.660
There are a number of
different labels supported.

00:33:21.660 --> 00:33:23.610
This is a subset that
I've listed here.

00:33:23.610 --> 00:33:26.370
The whole list would have
been totally illegible instead

00:33:26.370 --> 00:33:28.351
of slightly illegible.

00:33:28.351 --> 00:33:29.850
But this allows you
to break it down

00:33:29.850 --> 00:33:35.014
by method, by project, or by
service, or by response code.

00:33:35.014 --> 00:33:37.555
Maybe you're suddenly seeing an
uptick in a specific response

00:33:37.555 --> 00:33:39.240
code, and you want it to dig in.

00:33:39.240 --> 00:33:41.190
And then, as with all
metrics in Stackdriver,

00:33:41.190 --> 00:33:43.840
you can use group by or
filter to help refine, as Liz

00:33:43.840 --> 00:33:46.570
was talking about earlier.

00:33:46.570 --> 00:33:49.230
So how do you use these?

00:33:49.230 --> 00:33:51.120
What we expect you
to do with these

00:33:51.120 --> 00:33:54.180
is to look for correlated
SLI degradations.

00:33:54.180 --> 00:33:56.880
Your product's SLI is
trending in a bad direction?

00:33:56.880 --> 00:33:59.550
See if the GCP products
that it relies on

00:33:59.550 --> 00:34:02.280
are also having anomalies.

00:34:02.280 --> 00:34:03.840
Look at a baseline.

00:34:03.840 --> 00:34:06.440
See what it looked like last
week or the previous day,

00:34:06.440 --> 00:34:09.780
so that you know that it's
not some sort of cyclic thing

00:34:09.780 --> 00:34:13.409
that wouldn't necessarily lead
to an issue in your product.

00:34:13.409 --> 00:34:15.379
And use these to drive
support conversations.

00:34:15.379 --> 00:34:16.920
This is going to
help you have really

00:34:16.920 --> 00:34:18.960
efficient, effective
conversations with Google

00:34:18.960 --> 00:34:20.670
Support.

00:34:20.670 --> 00:34:22.110
Things not to do.

00:34:22.110 --> 00:34:25.199
Please don't assume this
is an SLA for Google.

00:34:25.199 --> 00:34:28.530
We are giving you this for
information to help you debug.

00:34:28.530 --> 00:34:30.090
It's not an SLA.

00:34:30.090 --> 00:34:32.616
And please don't call every
time there's an outlier.

00:34:32.616 --> 00:34:34.199
I'm sure you have
better things to do,

00:34:34.199 --> 00:34:36.880
and I'm sure support
folks do as well.

00:34:36.880 --> 00:34:39.570
And lastly, set up alerts
based on your SLIs.

00:34:39.570 --> 00:34:41.580
Don't set them up based on ours.

00:34:41.580 --> 00:34:43.650
We care our users
are having trouble,

00:34:43.650 --> 00:34:45.960
but you ought to care if your
users are having trouble,

00:34:45.960 --> 00:34:49.935
not if Google is
being a little wacky.

00:34:49.935 --> 00:34:51.560
If you'd like to
learn more about this,

00:34:51.560 --> 00:34:54.513
there's a demo area on the
first floor of Moscone West.

00:34:54.513 --> 00:34:56.429
If you go all the way
to the back of the room,

00:34:56.429 --> 00:34:58.640
we're near the folks
in the red shirts.

00:34:58.640 --> 00:35:00.240
Stackdriver PMs are
hanging out there

00:35:00.240 --> 00:35:02.970
for the whole conference,
so come talk to us.

00:35:02.970 --> 00:35:05.610
There's also a blog post coming
this week to the Google Cloud

00:35:05.610 --> 00:35:09.134
blog, where you can
learn a lot more.

00:35:09.134 --> 00:35:10.800
LIZ FONG-JONES: So
to conclude, today we

00:35:10.800 --> 00:35:12.630
talked about four
different things.

00:35:12.630 --> 00:35:15.330
We talked about why should
you adopt SRE techniques,

00:35:15.330 --> 00:35:17.640
how does SRE help solve
operational problems

00:35:17.640 --> 00:35:19.017
that you may be having.

00:35:19.017 --> 00:35:20.850
And then we talked about
three specific ways

00:35:20.850 --> 00:35:22.380
of applying these
SRE techniques,

00:35:22.380 --> 00:35:24.750
about how to measure your
success of your customers

00:35:24.750 --> 00:35:28.160
using SLOs, how do we
reduce her time to resolve

00:35:28.160 --> 00:35:31.770
and your time to detect
using SRE techniques,

00:35:31.770 --> 00:35:33.330
and how do we
debug problems that

00:35:33.330 --> 00:35:36.930
are somewhere between
Google and your own code

00:35:36.930 --> 00:35:40.050
by using transparent SLIs.

00:35:40.050 --> 00:35:44.250
So the key message that I want
you to walk away with today

00:35:44.250 --> 00:35:47.340
is that SRE techniques help you
solve your cloud reliability

00:35:47.340 --> 00:35:48.190
problems.

00:35:48.190 --> 00:35:52.260
It may be really easy to click
a button in the GCP console

00:35:52.260 --> 00:35:54.120
and get 10 times
as many servers.

00:35:54.120 --> 00:35:56.400
You can't get 10 times
as many operations staff

00:35:56.400 --> 00:35:57.820
at the click of a button.

00:35:57.820 --> 00:35:59.730
So it means that we
really need to invest

00:35:59.730 --> 00:36:02.040
in automation and
engineering for operability

00:36:02.040 --> 00:36:05.820
in order to be successful
in our cloud deployments.

00:36:05.820 --> 00:36:08.070
And furthermore, I want you
to walk away with the idea

00:36:08.070 --> 00:36:10.950
that having product
development and operations

00:36:10.950 --> 00:36:13.300
at each other's throats
is an unhealthy dynamic.

00:36:13.300 --> 00:36:14.790
It doesn't need to exist.

00:36:14.790 --> 00:36:16.530
That if you are
running your service

00:36:16.530 --> 00:36:21.840
to a same set of metrics, if
you have error budgets and SLOs,

00:36:21.840 --> 00:36:24.420
that it provides you with
common language and flexibility

00:36:24.420 --> 00:36:28.500
to decide how fast can I push
without impacting my users

00:36:28.500 --> 00:36:29.230
experience.

00:36:29.230 --> 00:36:32.760
How can I make sure they get
both adequate reliability

00:36:32.760 --> 00:36:35.700
and also shipping
our users' features

00:36:35.700 --> 00:36:38.352
as quickly as possible?

00:36:38.352 --> 00:36:39.810
The other thing I
wanted to mention

00:36:39.810 --> 00:36:42.240
is that GCP helps you, right?

00:36:42.240 --> 00:36:45.090
We're here to help you
achieve higher reliability.

00:36:45.090 --> 00:36:47.760
We're here to help you
achieve higher operability.

00:36:47.760 --> 00:36:49.980
And the way that we do
that is that we embed

00:36:49.980 --> 00:36:52.950
SRE concepts into Stackdriver
to help you operate more

00:36:52.950 --> 00:36:55.220
like an SRE and that
our products build

00:36:55.220 --> 00:36:57.510
in all of these
SRE best practices,

00:36:57.510 --> 00:36:59.580
so that you can have
lower cost of ownership,

00:36:59.580 --> 00:37:01.980
so that you can reduce the
tension between your teams,

00:37:01.980 --> 00:37:04.977
and so you can scale up.

00:37:04.977 --> 00:37:06.810
So I promised I'd tell
you a little bit more

00:37:06.810 --> 00:37:09.650
about my team, the Customer
Reliability Engineering Team.

00:37:09.650 --> 00:37:12.210
So we're a set of experienced
Google Site Reliability

00:37:12.210 --> 00:37:14.640
engineers, and we've
shifted our focus.

00:37:14.640 --> 00:37:17.340
Instead of focusing on
individual GCP products,

00:37:17.340 --> 00:37:20.040
we focus on helping our
customers be successful,

00:37:20.040 --> 00:37:22.202
helping our customers
be reliable.

00:37:22.202 --> 00:37:23.410
And what form does that take?

00:37:23.410 --> 00:37:26.880
Well, we help our customers set
service level objectives that

00:37:26.880 --> 00:37:28.950
are meaningful to their users.

00:37:28.950 --> 00:37:31.230
We help people do
reliability reviews.

00:37:31.230 --> 00:37:34.380
You can tell, is my
application engineered

00:37:34.380 --> 00:37:36.930
to meet these service level
objectives that I think

00:37:36.930 --> 00:37:38.586
will make my customers happy?

00:37:38.586 --> 00:37:39.960
And if not, we'll
give you a list

00:37:39.960 --> 00:37:42.270
of items that will
help you decide

00:37:42.270 --> 00:37:45.090
where do I want to invest my
time to make my system more

00:37:45.090 --> 00:37:46.830
operable.

00:37:46.830 --> 00:37:49.400
We also do shared
monitoring, right?

00:37:49.400 --> 00:37:52.770
So Marie told you about having
visibility of Google service

00:37:52.770 --> 00:37:55.764
level indicators to
you, the customer.

00:37:55.764 --> 00:37:58.180
However, we also think that
it's important to have support

00:37:58.180 --> 00:38:00.430
on the same page,
that it should be

00:38:00.430 --> 00:38:03.070
possible for you, as
a customer of GCP,

00:38:03.070 --> 00:38:05.260
to expose your service
level indicators

00:38:05.260 --> 00:38:08.530
and objectives to us,
so that support can see,

00:38:08.530 --> 00:38:11.700
on one page, your service
level indicator being impacted

00:38:11.700 --> 00:38:14.410
and a GCP service level
indicator being impacted and be

00:38:14.410 --> 00:38:17.749
able to debug and know
is the problem fixed

00:38:17.749 --> 00:38:19.790
or not without having to
call you up and ask you,

00:38:19.790 --> 00:38:21.010
is it better yet?

00:38:21.010 --> 00:38:22.510
So we do things
like shared learning

00:38:22.510 --> 00:38:25.210
as well, so that we'll
get proactively notified

00:38:25.210 --> 00:38:27.086
if a number of customers
start experiencing

00:38:27.086 --> 00:38:28.460
an issue with
their service level

00:38:28.460 --> 00:38:30.880
objectives in a
correlated fashion.

00:38:30.880 --> 00:38:33.700
We also help people that
are large customers--

00:38:33.700 --> 00:38:35.950
for instance, retailers--
with critical periods

00:38:35.950 --> 00:38:37.570
like Black Friday.

00:38:37.570 --> 00:38:39.580
And then we help you
with ongoing operations,

00:38:39.580 --> 00:38:41.760
things like doing a joint
post-mortem whenever

00:38:41.760 --> 00:38:42.790
there is a outage.

00:38:42.790 --> 00:38:44.610
Maybe there's something that
you could do to mitigate

00:38:44.610 --> 00:38:46.027
and that we could
do to mitigate.

00:38:46.027 --> 00:38:47.860
There's not a reason
to do only one of them.

00:38:47.860 --> 00:38:49.210
We should do both.

00:38:49.210 --> 00:38:51.764
And we help people be more
reliable all the time,

00:38:51.764 --> 00:38:53.680
whether it be looking
at your design documents

00:38:53.680 --> 00:38:57.190
or helping you do
disaster drills.

00:38:57.190 --> 00:39:00.700
So you can learn more about
SRE, both from reading the Site

00:39:00.700 --> 00:39:03.040
Reliability Engineering
book, the first one,

00:39:03.040 --> 00:39:07.270
which is available for free
online at google.com/sre.

00:39:07.270 --> 00:39:09.670
You can also go and pay us
a visit at the Fifth Nine

00:39:09.670 --> 00:39:13.450
Lounge, which is located on the
second floor of Moscone West.

00:39:13.450 --> 00:39:16.300
And there, we're distributing
a book, print copies of a book

00:39:16.300 --> 00:39:18.970
called The Site Reliability
Workbook, which just came out

00:39:18.970 --> 00:39:20.500
today fresh off the presses.

00:39:20.500 --> 00:39:23.300
We'll even sign a copy for you.

00:39:23.300 --> 00:39:25.030
And furthermore,
there's a book coming up

00:39:25.030 --> 00:39:26.800
in the next month
called Seeking SRE,

00:39:26.800 --> 00:39:29.290
which was written by a person
who doesn't work at Google.

00:39:29.290 --> 00:39:31.420
And it's a compendium of
industry best practices,

00:39:31.420 --> 00:39:33.520
both from Google and
from outside of Google.

00:39:33.520 --> 00:39:35.710
The Site Reliability Workbook
also includes lessons

00:39:35.710 --> 00:39:37.730
from our customers.

00:39:37.730 --> 00:39:40.030
Another place you may
want to go to learn more

00:39:40.030 --> 00:39:45.160
is to attend Jess and Anne's
talk at 4:45 PM tomorrow.

00:39:45.160 --> 00:39:48.490
I believe it is in Moscone West.

00:39:48.490 --> 00:39:51.140
So with that, we have 10
minutes for questions.

00:39:51.140 --> 00:39:53.470
So please raise your
hand, and someone

00:39:53.470 --> 00:39:54.940
will bring a microphone around.

00:39:54.940 --> 00:39:57.280
And we'll try to answer
any questions that we can

00:39:57.280 --> 00:39:58.490
with the remaining time.

00:39:58.490 --> 00:40:00.524
Thank you very much
for listening to us.

