00:42:43.030 --> 00:42:44.470
with adopting their SRE culture.

00:42:47.190 --> 00:42:48.660
Over here in the front.

00:42:48.660 --> 00:42:51.600
AUDIENCE: So my customers--

00:42:51.600 --> 00:42:53.010
it's not about uptime.

00:42:53.010 --> 00:42:56.667
It's about serving X amount
of requests by Y date.

00:42:56.667 --> 00:42:57.500
LIZ FONG-JONES: Yup.

00:42:57.500 --> 00:43:00.510
AUDIENCE: So how
would I design metrics

00:43:00.510 --> 00:43:03.090
around, hey, we're getting
close, and this is dangerous?

00:43:03.090 --> 00:43:05.340
LIZ FONG-JONES: That is a
difference between a service

00:43:05.340 --> 00:43:07.842
level objective and a
service level agreement

00:43:07.842 --> 00:43:09.300
is that you want
your service level

00:43:09.300 --> 00:43:11.940
objective to be set more
aggressively than your service

00:43:11.940 --> 00:43:12.730
level agreement.

00:43:12.730 --> 00:43:14.700
So you will get
paged before you run

00:43:14.700 --> 00:43:16.260
into the danger
zone and fall afoul

00:43:16.260 --> 00:43:18.520
of your legal obligations.

00:43:18.520 --> 00:43:21.160
So you might want to
set, for instance,

00:43:21.160 --> 00:43:25.440
a service a little objective on
throughput with the idea being

00:43:25.440 --> 00:43:27.996
that if your service level
objective starts degrading,

00:43:27.996 --> 00:43:29.370
like, if you have
your throughput

00:43:29.370 --> 00:43:31.320
degraded below the level
that you would need in order

00:43:31.320 --> 00:43:33.840
to complete everything in
the requisite amount of time,

00:43:33.840 --> 00:43:35.440
that you would start
getting alerted about it.

00:43:35.440 --> 00:43:36.065
Does that help?

00:43:36.065 --> 00:43:37.590
AUDIENCE: Yes, a lot.

00:43:37.590 --> 00:43:39.237
LIZ FONG-JONES: Great.

00:43:39.237 --> 00:43:40.320
AUDIENCE: Oh, that's loud.

00:43:40.320 --> 00:43:41.050
Sorry.

00:43:41.050 --> 00:43:45.000
So you mentioned alerting
on SLO violations

00:43:45.000 --> 00:43:47.550
instead of alerting
on root causes.

00:43:47.550 --> 00:43:49.960
In my experience, root causes.

00:43:49.960 --> 00:43:53.437
sometimes are leading
indicators of SLO violations.

00:43:53.437 --> 00:43:54.270
LIZ FONG-JONES: Yes.

00:43:54.270 --> 00:43:55.811
AUDIENCE: How do
you feel about that?

00:43:55.811 --> 00:43:58.200
And how do you approach that?

00:43:58.200 --> 00:44:02.160
LIZ FONG-JONES: If a root
cause is very, very, very

00:44:02.160 --> 00:44:05.190
strongly correlated in
an advanced predictor,

00:44:05.190 --> 00:44:07.410
then it winds up sometimes
being a good idea

00:44:07.410 --> 00:44:08.770
to alert in advance.

00:44:08.770 --> 00:44:11.530
However, most of the
time that is not true.

00:44:11.530 --> 00:44:15.270
Most of people's
potential causes

00:44:15.270 --> 00:44:17.100
are in fact only potential.

00:44:17.100 --> 00:44:20.460
So I would say it's probably
OK to have maybe three or four

00:44:20.460 --> 00:44:21.344
potential causes.

00:44:21.344 --> 00:44:23.010
It's probably not OK
to have 100, right?

00:44:23.010 --> 00:44:25.200
You kind of have to have
balance in all things.

00:44:25.200 --> 00:44:28.050
At the end of the day, it
also depends upon your window

00:44:28.050 --> 00:44:29.520
to react, right?

00:44:29.520 --> 00:44:33.210
If you have a three-nines SLA,
that gives you enough time

00:44:33.210 --> 00:44:35.520
to investigate something.

00:44:35.520 --> 00:44:37.970
And if you have a
five-nines SLA--

00:44:37.970 --> 00:44:39.870
or sorry, if you have
a five-nines SLO,

00:44:39.870 --> 00:44:42.150
maybe you're better off
implementing some kind

00:44:42.150 --> 00:44:44.490
of automation to deal
with the condition

00:44:44.490 --> 00:44:47.520
that reliably, predictably
leads to degradation

00:44:47.520 --> 00:44:48.270
in service, right?

00:44:48.270 --> 00:44:50.460
You would rather write a
robot to do that for you

00:44:50.460 --> 00:44:53.886
rather than alert you with,
hey, this thing's in danger,

00:44:53.886 --> 00:44:55.260
and it always
leads to a problem,

00:44:55.260 --> 00:44:56.190
and you always do
the same thing.

00:44:56.190 --> 00:44:57.731
Why not have a robot
do that for you?

00:45:00.770 --> 00:45:01.932
Other questions.

00:45:01.932 --> 00:45:02.890
Over here in the front.

00:45:04.782 --> 00:45:06.240
MARIA COSGROVE-DAVIES:
It's coming.

00:45:06.240 --> 00:45:07.030
LIZ FONG-JONES:
Yeah, it's coming.

00:45:07.030 --> 00:45:07.996
It's coming.

00:45:11.380 --> 00:45:14.730
AUDIENCE: So there are
a lot of Google services

00:45:14.730 --> 00:45:19.140
that we use where there is
a time for data propagation.

00:45:19.140 --> 00:45:22.567
So for example, when you add
a user to a group in G Suite--

00:45:22.567 --> 00:45:23.400
LIZ FONG-JONES: Yup.

00:45:23.400 --> 00:45:24.900
AUDIENCE: --or when
you change and I

00:45:24.900 --> 00:45:28.350
am role or something like that,
it says it could take a minute

00:45:28.350 --> 00:45:29.910
or it could take up to 24 hours.

00:45:29.910 --> 00:45:34.500
How do you decide
your internal targets

00:45:34.500 --> 00:45:38.640
versus what you say in
the documentation, what

00:45:38.640 --> 00:45:39.780
that delta really is?

00:45:39.780 --> 00:45:41.100
LIZ FONG-JONES: That's a
product management thing.

00:45:41.100 --> 00:45:41.460
Marie.

00:45:41.460 --> 00:45:43.293
MARIA COSGROVE-DAVIES:
[LAUGHS] Thanks, Liz.

00:45:43.293 --> 00:45:44.787
You're a real pal.

00:45:44.787 --> 00:45:46.620
It's going to vary
depending on the service.

00:45:46.620 --> 00:45:48.510
And what it will depend
on is exactly what

00:45:48.510 --> 00:45:50.134
we talked about,
which is what we think

00:45:50.134 --> 00:45:51.390
the users are looking for.

00:45:51.390 --> 00:45:53.170
So for something like
adding an I am role,

00:45:53.170 --> 00:45:55.470
you probably expect a
much quicker turnaround.

00:45:55.470 --> 00:45:57.780
Whereas if you're
sending logs to a storage

00:45:57.780 --> 00:46:00.977
bucket for cold storage,
then you can wait.

00:46:00.977 --> 00:46:03.060
AUDIENCE: Yeah, I'm more
thinking what the gap is,

00:46:03.060 --> 00:46:03.559
though.

00:46:03.559 --> 00:46:05.730
So a lot of times, we
see that a propagation

00:46:05.730 --> 00:46:07.140
could take up to 24 hours.

00:46:07.140 --> 00:46:07.350
MARIA COSGROVE-DAVIES: Right.

00:46:07.350 --> 00:46:09.630
AUDIENCE: But the reality
is it's, like, five minutes.

00:46:09.630 --> 00:46:10.985
So there's a huge gap between--

00:46:10.985 --> 00:46:11.280
LIZ FONG-JONES: Ah, yes.

00:46:11.280 --> 00:46:11.860
That's a fascinating question.

00:46:11.860 --> 00:46:13.000
AUDIENCE: --what's normal
and t hen what we--

00:46:13.000 --> 00:46:14.850
LIZ FONG-JONES: Yeah,
we love that question.

00:46:14.850 --> 00:46:17.310
So that is an example
where the service level

00:46:17.310 --> 00:46:20.970
objective and the
real user experience

00:46:20.970 --> 00:46:22.920
have diverged from each other.

00:46:22.920 --> 00:46:25.032
And in those cases,
it is usually

00:46:25.032 --> 00:46:26.490
a thing we recommend
as an SRE best

00:46:26.490 --> 00:46:28.960
practice-- sorry to make more
work for you, Marie, right?

00:46:28.960 --> 00:46:30.751
But we usually recommend
as a best practice

00:46:30.751 --> 00:46:34.290
to either make sure that
the SLOs reflect reality

00:46:34.290 --> 00:46:37.565
or to degrade reality in order
to match the SLOs, right?

00:46:37.565 --> 00:46:38.940
In order to make
sure that people

00:46:38.940 --> 00:46:41.490
get predictable,
consistent performance.

00:46:41.490 --> 00:46:45.030
So yes, you're right to
note that that is a gap.

00:46:45.030 --> 00:46:46.942
And that is something
where we probably

00:46:46.942 --> 00:46:48.900
should either tighten
one or loosen one, right?

00:46:48.900 --> 00:46:51.380
Like when you have
an aspirational SLO

00:46:51.380 --> 00:46:54.000
and an actual,
on-the-ground SLO that

00:46:54.000 --> 00:46:56.550
are de facto different from
each other, that's a problem.

00:46:56.550 --> 00:46:57.720
AUDIENCE: Yeah, you
typically wouldn't

00:46:57.720 --> 00:46:59.790
expect them to be orders
of magnitude apart.

00:46:59.790 --> 00:47:02.610
LIZ FONG-JONES: Exactly, not
orders of magnitude apart.

00:47:02.610 --> 00:47:03.720
MARIA COSGROVE-DAVIES: We'll
go ahead and make that slower

00:47:03.720 --> 00:47:04.219
then.

00:47:04.219 --> 00:47:07.231
[LAUGHTER]

00:47:10.010 --> 00:47:12.080
LIZ FONG-JONES: Any
other questions?

00:47:12.080 --> 00:47:12.890
One more question.

00:47:12.890 --> 00:47:14.390
We've got two minutes left.

00:47:14.390 --> 00:47:18.140
AUDIENCE: So you talked about
choosing your SLIs to match

00:47:18.140 --> 00:47:22.800
what your users care about.

00:47:22.800 --> 00:47:27.313
Is there value in trying to
go beyond just an HTTP 500

00:47:27.313 --> 00:47:30.670
in that sometimes
a 400 is actually

00:47:30.670 --> 00:47:33.140
a problem in our client,
that the user is--

00:47:33.140 --> 00:47:35.150
it's still a
problem to the user,

00:47:35.150 --> 00:47:37.880
but those can be a lot harder
to detect and understand

00:47:37.880 --> 00:47:39.582
in a useful way.

00:47:39.582 --> 00:47:41.540
LIZ FONG-JONES: That gets
to Marie's discussion

00:47:41.540 --> 00:47:44.600
of measuring user journeys
rather than single requests.

00:47:44.600 --> 00:47:47.690
So if you can measure an entire
user journey-- for instance,

00:47:47.690 --> 00:47:49.700
measuring not
just, you know, did

00:47:49.700 --> 00:47:52.040
loading the home page of
my shopping website work,

00:47:52.040 --> 00:47:54.890
but was I, in fact, able
to add an item to a cart

00:47:54.890 --> 00:47:55.861
and check out?

00:47:55.861 --> 00:47:57.860
That's going to be a much
more robust situation.

00:47:57.860 --> 00:48:00.190
So the same thing goes
for HTTP 400s, right?

00:48:00.190 --> 00:48:03.500
Like are users experiencing
what they are expecting to see?

00:48:03.500 --> 00:48:06.440
Or are users expecting
an abnormal condition?

00:48:06.440 --> 00:48:09.440
And that's something that
you need to make sure that--

00:48:09.440 --> 00:48:12.855
the best possible SLO is
a critical user journey,

00:48:12.855 --> 00:48:14.480
but you have to start
somewhere, right?

00:48:14.480 --> 00:48:16.430
An imperfect SLO is
better than no SLO.

00:48:16.430 --> 00:48:18.140
So start measuring,
otherwise you're

00:48:18.140 --> 00:48:20.020
just taking stabs in the dark.

00:48:20.020 --> 00:48:21.050
MARIA COSGROVE-DAVIES:
And so a better framing

00:48:21.050 --> 00:48:23.060
of that might be
something that's not a 200

00:48:23.060 --> 00:48:27.830
would be a failure rather
than a 500 is a failure.

00:48:27.830 --> 00:48:28.520
All right.

00:48:28.520 --> 00:48:29.210
LIZ FONG-JONES: Excellent.

00:48:29.210 --> 00:48:30.330
Thank you all for coming.

00:48:30.330 --> 00:48:31.965
And if you want to
find us afterwards,

00:48:31.965 --> 00:48:33.340
you can find us
at the Fifth Nine

00:48:33.340 --> 00:48:35.720
on the second floor
of Moscone West.

00:48:35.720 --> 00:48:37.220
MARIA COSGROVE-DAVIES:
Thanks a lot.

00:48:37.220 --> 00:48:38.720
[APPLAUSE]

00:48:38.720 --> 00:48:42.070
[MUSIC PLAYING]

